    <h1>Web Hosting Reviews and Comparisons</h1>
    <p>
        Choosing the right web hosting service is important for website
        performance, reliability and security. This guide explains common
        hosting technologies and what to consider before choosing a provider.
    </p>

    <section>
        <h2>1. What Is Web Hosting?</h2>
        <p>
            Web hosting is a service that stores website files and makes
            them accessible through the internet. Hosting providers supply
            the servers and infrastructure needed to publish websites.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>2. Types of Web Hosting</h2>

        <h3>Shared Hosting</h3>
        <p>
            Multiple websites share resources on one server. It is often
            suitable for beginners and small websites, but performance can
            be affected by other websites using the same resources.
        </p>

        <h3>VPS Hosting</h3>
        <p>
            Virtual Private Server hosting provides an isolated portion
            of a physical server. It offers more control and dedicated
            resource allocations than typical shared hosting.
        </p>

        <h3>Cloud Hosting</h3>
        <p>
            Cloud hosting uses a network of connected servers. Depending
            on the provider and configuration, it can offer flexibility
            and scalability as website traffic changes.
        </p>

        <h3>Dedicated Server Hosting</h3>
        <p>
            A dedicated server gives one customer access to an entire
            physical server. It offers greater control but usually
            requires more technical knowledge and administration.
        </p>

        <h3>Managed WordPress Hosting</h3>
        <p>
            This hosting is designed for WordPress websites. Providers
            may handle tasks such as updates, backups, caching and
            WordPress-specific security.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>3. Website Performance</h2>
        <p>
            Hosting performance can affect loading speed and visitor
            experience. Consider server response time, storage technology,
            bandwidth, caching, server location and uptime history.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>4. Hosting Security</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>SSL/TLS certificates and HTTPS support.</li>
            <li>Regular backups and recovery options.</li>
            <li>Server and software security updates.</li>
            <li>Firewall and malware protection.</li>
            <li>Account security and multi-factor authentication.</li>
            <li>Clear incident response and support procedures.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>5. How to Compare Hosting Providers</h2>
        <p>Before choosing a hosting provider, compare:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Introductory price and renewal price.</li>
            <li>Storage, bandwidth and resource limits.</li>
            <li>Uptime commitments and service history.</li>
            <li>Backup availability and restoration costs.</li>
            <li>Customer support availability.</li>
            <li>Data centre locations and migration options.</li>
            <li>Security features and account protections.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Read the terms carefully. A low introductory price does not
            always mean the service will be inexpensive over time.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>6. Is Free Hosting a Good Choice?</h2>
        <p>
            Free hosting can be useful for learning, experiments and
            simple projects. However, it may have limitations involving
            storage, bandwidth, support, custom domains and server control.
            Review the provider's terms before using it for an important
            website.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>7. Responsible Hosting Choices</h2>
        <p>
            Choose a hosting service that supports secure website
            administration, regular maintenance, data protection and
            reliable backups. Keep website software updated and use
            strong, unique passwords for hosting accounts.
        </p>
    </section>

